    "title": "Lumens, brightness, and why more isn't better",
    "body": "\nIf you take one thing from this site, make it this: **lumens measure how much light a fixture puts out, not how well you can see.** Brightness and visibility are two different things.\n\n## The vocabulary, in plain English\n\n- **Lumen (lm)** — the total amount of light a bulb emits in every direction. A single candle is roughly 12 lumens; a classic 60-watt incandescent bulb is about 800 lumens.\n- **Lux** — lumens *per square meter*. Lux is what actually reaches a surface. A 10,000-lumen lamp pointed at the sky puts close to zero useful lux on the ground where you need it.\n- **Candela** — intensity in a single direction. A bare bulb glaring straight into your eye can have enormous candela and terrible usefulness.\n- **Color temperature (Kelvin, K)** — how \"warm\" or \"cool\" the light *looks*. Warm amber is below 3000K; neutral white is 3500–4000K; harsh blue-white \"daylight\" is above 4000K. The higher the Kelvin, the more blue light it emits — and the worse it is for people and wildlife at night.\n\n## What's actually on your street\n\nA typical modern street light runs **1,000 to 4,000 lumens**. That's not inherently evil. The problem is threefold:\n\n1. **It points everywhere.** An unshielded \"cobra-head\" or globe fixture throws a large share of its lumens up and sideways — lighting the sky, not the road.\n2. **It's too blue.** Many municipal LEDs are 4000K or 5000K. Blue-rich light scatters far more in the atmosphere (that's why the daytime sky is blue), so it drives skyglow out of proportion to its brightness.\n3. **It's on when nobody needs it.** Full output at 3 a.m. on an empty street wastes energy and does real biological harm for zero benefit.\n\n## The fix is measurement, not darkness\n\nThe goal is not fewer lumens — it's the *right* lumens, *aimed* at the ground, in a *warm* color, *dimmed* when the street is empty. A fully-shielded 3000K fixture at 2,000 lumens can light a road better than a 4,000K unshielded fixture at 4,000 lumens, while polluting a fraction of the sky.\n\nWhen you hear a city claim \"new LEDs are brighter and safer,\" ask a better question: **how many of those lumens are landing on the road, and what color are they?**\n"
